# Forecasting artifacts are version-controlled through Git LFS.
#
# These were deliberately untracked until now (see the note in .gitignore for
# what changed). They are ~214 MB across 747 files, so committing the bytes
# into git proper would roughly quadruple a 68 MB history permanently — and a
# later removal means rewriting and force-pushing main. LFS keeps the pointers
# in history and the payload out of it.
#
# Only the binary payloads are tracked here. The JSON beside each model
# (metadata, metrics, feature_columns) stays in plain git: it is small, it is
# text, and it is the part worth reading in a diff when a retrain moves a
# score.
#
# Anyone cloning this repo needs `git lfs install` before the checkout resolves
# these to real files rather than to pointer stubs.
# Shell scripts run inside Linux containers — CRLF from a Windows checkout
# breaks the shebang (`exec: no such file or directory`). Force LF in the
# working tree regardless of core.autocrlf.
*.sh text eol=lf

*.pkl filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.nc filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
backend/models/forecasting/_reports/**/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# machine_learning/models/ (trained artifacts, .joblib + .pt) and
# machine_learning/exports/ (.nc prediction grids the backend serves as map
# layers) — same reasoning as above, extended to the offline-ML side: a fresh
# clone or `docker compose build` needs exports/ to exist without first
# running the ML pipeline, and models/ is what regenerates it.
*.joblib filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.pt filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
